    "id": "2026-07-17T23:10:34.893Z",
    "title": "Word Selection Livestream",
    "filename": "Word Selection Livestream",
    "content": "Intro\n\nHey everyone, welcome to the stream! Today, we are starting a series to expand the vocabulary for the constructed language, Kalob.\n\nNow, originally, I set up a poll for us to vote on the word shapes for our new roots. I love democracy, but unfortunately, democracy with exactly two people voting mostly just leads to a lot of dead ties. So, instead of flipping a coin, I decided to leave it up to the Wheel of Destiny.\n\n[Visual: Mouse cursor hovers over or wiggles the spinning wheel on screen.]\n\nHere is how this is going to work. For each new root concept we need, I have a few different word shape options derived from real-world source languages. I’m going to introduce the source word, try my very best to pronounce it correctly—please forgive me in advance for butchering anything—and then show you what that word looks like adapted into Kalob’s phonology.\n\nYou’ll notice the slices on this wheel aren't equal. The weighting here is actually mathematical. I inverted the proportion of existing Kalob words derived from these source languages. Basically: the fewer words we currently have from a specific language, the bigger its slice on the wheel. We want to prioritize languages that have less coverage so Kalob's lexicon stays beautifully diverse.\n\nAlright, let’s get into the first word!\n\nWord\n\n[Visual: The tab of the browser is switched to show the slices for the first word concept.]\n\nOur first concept for today is the word for [English meaning]. Here are the options:\n\nOur first option comes from [Source Language A]. The original word is [Source Word A]—pronounced roughly like [Attempt pronunciation]. Adapted to fit Kalob's phonotactics, the word shape becomes [Kalob Shape A].\nBecause we already have a decent chunk of vocabulary from [Source Language A], it only gets a [30%] slice on the wheel.\n\nOur second option comes from [Source Language B]. The source word is [Source Word B], pronounced [Attempt pronunciation]. Adapted into Kalob, this becomes [Kalob Shape B].\nWe barely have any roots from [Source Language B] yet, so it’s getting the lion's share of the wheel today with a [70%] slice.\n\n[Visual: Streamer clicks the \"Spin\" button. The wheel ticks loudly as it spins.]\n\nLet's see what the lexicon demands... Come on, wheel...\n\n[Visual: Wheel slows down and stops on the winning slice. Confetti or flashing effect on screen if applicable.]\n\nAnd there we go! The winner is [Winning Kalob Shape], derived from [Winning Source Language]! I actually really like the way that sounds. Let's make it official and see how it looks in action.\n\n[Visual: Text on screen updates to show the winning word and a couple of sentence examples.]\n\nHere is [Winning Kalob Shape] used in a couple of target sentences to get a feel for the syntax.\n\nFirst, we have:\n'[Read Kalob Sentence 1]'\nWhich translates to: '[English Translation 1]'.\n\nAnd for a slightly more complex use:\n'[Read Kalob Sentence 2]'\nWhich means: '[English Translation 2]'.\n\nAwesome. That fits perfectly into the dictionary. Let's move on to the next tie-breaker!\n\n[Return to the start of the Word section for each additional word]\n\nOutro\n\n[Visual: The wheel is cleared away, perhaps showing a list of all the new words generated during the stream.]",
    "created": 1783514393962,
    "modified": 1784329834893,
    "compression": false,
    "compressionLevel": 1,
